WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:02.634
-They love you. We love you.
Welcome to the show.

00:00:02.634 --> 00:00:04.467
Thanks so much for coming on.
-Well, thank you

00:00:04.467 --> 00:00:06.467
for not making me put
a toilet plunger on my head.

00:00:06.467 --> 00:00:09.033
-Well, the night is young.
-I watched the segment.

00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:12.067
I was like, "Oh, my God.
What does he do on this show?"

00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:13.400
-He's a plumber in the --

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.067
I have -- I have
a thousand questions.

00:00:16.067 --> 00:00:18.701
I wanted to ask you
a zillion things.

00:00:18.701 --> 00:00:20.834
But, of course,
everyone's talking about this.

00:00:20.834 --> 00:00:23.267
The biggest news story
right now. Trump got indicted.

00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:26.901
-Yes.
-And is -- what do we know

00:00:26.901 --> 00:00:30.067
so far and what do we think
is going to happen?

00:00:30.067 --> 00:00:32.033
-Well, we don't know a lot
because the indictment

00:00:32.033 --> 00:00:33.167
is still sealed.
-Right.

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:34.801
-But we've heard
that there are reports

00:00:34.801 --> 00:00:37.234
there are as many as 30 charges
in the indictment.

00:00:37.234 --> 00:00:40.968
It will be unsealed on Tuesday
afternoon, around 2:00, 2:15,

00:00:40.968 --> 00:00:44.667
when former President Trump
will be arraigned,

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:46.300
which is an historic first.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.667
It has never before happened

00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:50.300
in the history
of the United States of America.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.200
And he will be taken downtown
to be processed.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:57.067
He will be fingerprinted.
They will take a mugshot.

00:00:57.067 --> 00:00:58.901
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.901 --> 00:01:00.467
It is...

00:01:00.467 --> 00:01:02.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:02.167 --> 00:01:05.400
-Is this a bizarre time to be --

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:07.033
You're premiering your show
on Monday.

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.467
-We are.

00:01:08.467 --> 00:01:10.334
And it's an incredible
news cycle to be working in.

00:01:10.334 --> 00:01:11.901
And, you know,
this show and this --

00:01:11.901 --> 00:01:13.434
It's a brand-new
cable-news channel --

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:14.834
like we need another one.
I know.

00:01:14.834 --> 00:01:16.501
Everybody's like,
"We got another one? Seriously?"

00:01:16.501 --> 00:01:18.868
-Yeah.
-But it's a good one.

00:01:18.868 --> 00:01:21.300
And -- And -- I promise.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.968
Because I think right now
on cable news,

00:01:22.968 --> 00:01:25.167
you see a lot of shouting
talking heads

00:01:25.167 --> 00:01:26.634
on the far right
and the far left.

00:01:26.634 --> 00:01:29.667
And I think the 80% of America
is in the middle somehow

00:01:29.667 --> 00:01:31.300
in the forgotten majority.

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:32.334
-Yeah, and we get to see --

00:01:32.334 --> 00:01:33.701
[ Applause ]

00:01:33.701 --> 00:01:35.601
We get to see Elizabeth Vargas.

00:01:35.601 --> 00:01:36.868
You are one of my favorites.

00:01:36.868 --> 00:01:39.934
You are an incredible
journalist, award-winning.

00:01:39.934 --> 00:01:41.100
Did you always want to be on TV?

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.067
Just hearing your voice,
by the way, I go --

00:01:43.067 --> 00:01:44.868
I get chills. You're so good.

00:01:44.868 --> 00:01:46.567
-[ Laughs ] You're so funny.
And you're so full of it.

00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:47.868
Come on.
-I'm not. I swear!

00:01:47.868 --> 00:01:50.667
I'm a giant --
By the way, backstage talking

00:01:50.667 --> 00:01:53.133
is different than this voice.
-Oh, please. Anyway...

00:01:53.133 --> 00:01:56.200
No, I did not always
want to be --

00:01:56.200 --> 00:01:57.667
-That's not how
you talked backstage.

00:01:57.667 --> 00:01:59.601
You're like, "Hi, Jimmy!"

00:01:59.601 --> 00:02:02.067
[ Laughter ]

00:02:02.067 --> 00:02:04.567
"Hi-eeeee!"
-I do not sound like a chipmunk.

00:02:04.567 --> 00:02:07.234
-No.
But you're very professional.

00:02:07.234 --> 00:02:08.601
No, you know how to do it.

00:02:08.601 --> 00:02:10.367
This clip is going around
the Internet now.

00:02:10.367 --> 00:02:12.667
This is from back
on the "Today" show in 1994.

00:02:12.667 --> 00:02:14.968
-Oh, yeah.
-Everyone was trying

00:02:14.968 --> 00:02:18.133
to figure out this new thing
called the Internet.

00:02:18.133 --> 00:02:19.467
-Yeah.
-Take a look at you

00:02:19.467 --> 00:02:21.133
and Bryant Gumbel
and Katie Couric

00:02:21.133 --> 00:02:23.968
trying to figure out
what the Internet is.

00:02:23.968 --> 00:02:26.968
-That little mark with the "A"
and then the ring around it.

00:02:26.968 --> 00:02:28.567
-"At"?
-See, that's what I said.

00:02:28.567 --> 00:02:29.968
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:29.968 --> 00:02:31.734
-Katie said she thought
it was "about."

00:02:31.734 --> 00:02:33.968
What is Internet anyway?

00:02:33.968 --> 00:02:38.000
-Internet is that massive
computer network,

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:39.968
the one that's becoming
really big now.

00:02:39.968 --> 00:02:41.767
A lot of people use it
and communicate.

00:02:41.767 --> 00:02:44.434
I guess they can communicate
with NBC writers and producers.

00:02:44.434 --> 00:02:47.167
Allison, can you explain
what Internet is?

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:49.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.968 --> 00:02:51.400
-"Can you explain
what Internet is?"

00:02:51.400 --> 00:02:54.200
Isn't it wild to watch it now?

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:56.834
-That clip gets resurrected
about every four months

00:02:56.834 --> 00:02:58.667
on Twitter and social media,
and people are like,

00:02:58.667 --> 00:03:00.067
"Oh, my God!
Have you seen this?"

00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:02.234
It was like,
"Yes, about 49,000 times."

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:04.167
-Yeah.
-But here's the funny story.

00:03:04.167 --> 00:03:06.267
First of all, my kids --
I've got two teenage sons.

00:03:06.267 --> 00:03:07.834
They took a look at it,
and they were like,

00:03:07.834 --> 00:03:09.367
"Mom, you're the one

00:03:09.367 --> 00:03:11.200
who's explaining
what the Internet is?"

00:03:11.200 --> 00:03:13.601
-Yeah. "You know nothing
about the Internet!"

00:03:13.601 --> 00:03:15.801
-Yeah.
-Yeah. That's so wild.

00:03:15.801 --> 00:03:17.133
-But you know what?

00:03:17.133 --> 00:03:21.467
BMW used that clip for a huge
Super Bowl commercial.

00:03:21.467 --> 00:03:23.667
-Oh.
-And they cut me out!

00:03:23.667 --> 00:03:25.667
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.667 --> 00:03:29.467
It was only Bryant and Katie,
and they got free BMWs.

00:03:29.467 --> 00:03:31.534
-Wait! What?!

00:03:31.534 --> 00:03:33.000
-I got completely shafted!

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.634
-We can make this --
No, we can make this all good.

00:03:35.634 --> 00:03:36.868
BMW, if you're watching.
-Yes.

00:03:36.868 --> 00:03:38.601
-First of all, I'm the one
who brought it up.

00:03:38.601 --> 00:03:40.267
-Yes.
-So I think...

00:03:40.267 --> 00:03:42.067
[ Laughter ]

00:03:42.067 --> 00:03:43.701
[ Applause ]

00:03:43.701 --> 00:03:45.434
And you all were here
to witness it.

00:03:45.434 --> 00:03:50.334
You all deserve cars.
Everyone here deserves a BMW!

00:03:50.334 --> 00:03:54.133
Come on! Make it right.
Make it right, BMW.

00:03:54.133 --> 00:03:55.934
-You're the new Oprah.
-Thank you so much. Yeah.

00:03:55.934 --> 00:03:57.400
-BMWs for everyone.
-Yeah.

00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:00.300
No one's getting a car at all.

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:02.367
You know, we were talking
backstage and I go --

00:04:02.367 --> 00:04:04.501
You know, you've interviewed
every single person

00:04:04.501 --> 00:04:06.434
that I want to go --
There's such a giant list.

00:04:06.434 --> 00:04:08.267
But I just picked two
that stuck in my head.

00:04:08.267 --> 00:04:09.801
I go, "I'd love
to hear the story."

00:04:09.801 --> 00:04:11.701
Tell me about when
you interviewed Mick Jagger,

00:04:11.701 --> 00:04:13.601
who's one of my all-time faves.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:04:13.601 --> 00:04:15.634
-Oh, you got the picture!
-Yeah, Look at --

00:04:15.634 --> 00:04:18.100
-Yeah, look at that.
-[As Jagger] What was he like?

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.167
-You know, first of all,
he hardly ever gives interviews.

00:04:20.167 --> 00:04:22.868
-No, he doesn't like
to talk with people. Yeah.

00:04:22.868 --> 00:04:25.000
-I could have just
interviewed you.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:27.968
-That's right. Where were we?

00:04:27.968 --> 00:04:29.667
-You gotta get
a couple of wrinkles.

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:31.067
-Yeah. We got to do like that.

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:33.100
[Normal voice] What did you say
to him? Where were you?

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:35.767
-Well, here's the big thing.
We were both --

00:04:35.767 --> 00:04:37.133
I was interviewing him in LA,

00:04:37.133 --> 00:04:40.033
and I was staying in the
Four Seasons in Beverly Hills

00:04:40.033 --> 00:04:41.501
in a tiny little room.

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.734
And he was staying at the
Four Seasons in Beverly Hills

00:04:43.734 --> 00:04:45.133
in the massive
presidential suite,

00:04:45.133 --> 00:04:46.801
which is the entire top floor
of the hotel.

00:04:46.801 --> 00:04:49.601
-Yeah.
-And some producer in New York

00:04:49.601 --> 00:04:52.501
who I didn't --
hadn't worked with before

00:04:52.501 --> 00:04:54.467
decided to e-mail me,
fax me --

00:04:54.467 --> 00:04:56.133
That was -- Fax me.

00:04:56.133 --> 00:04:57.534
Some questions that she thought

00:04:57.534 --> 00:04:59.000
would be good
to ask Mick Jagger.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.133
-"What is the Internet?"
-I know.

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:02.400
"What is the Internet?"

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:03.901
-"What is a fax machine?"
-"What is a fax machine?"

00:05:03.901 --> 00:05:06.634
-The concierge of the hotel
took them

00:05:06.634 --> 00:05:10.300
and put them under Mick Jagger's
door instead of my door.

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.868
And the questions were bad.

00:05:13.868 --> 00:05:16.400
And I was awakened
the morning of the interview

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.868
with a panicky phone call from
the booker in New York saying,

00:05:18.868 --> 00:05:21.434
"You've got to go straight
away up to Mick Jagger's

00:05:21.434 --> 00:05:23.767
presidential suite
and make this right

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:27.367
and tell him that this was
some rogue producer in New York

00:05:27.367 --> 00:05:28.968
who didn't know
what she was doing."

00:05:28.968 --> 00:05:30.667
-Because you're not even
allowed to give questions.

00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:33.234
-No.
We never give questions out.

00:05:33.234 --> 00:05:35.100
But I never -- I don't even
write down questions

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:36.367
when I do interviews.

00:05:36.367 --> 00:05:37.934
I mean, I would never have
asked those questions

00:05:37.934 --> 00:05:39.734
that were being faxed.
They were not good questions.

00:05:39.734 --> 00:05:41.133
-Ooh.
-So they said, "You've got

00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:42.868
to go up there right away,
and I was like, "Oh, my God."

00:05:42.868 --> 00:05:44.601
-He canceled. He said --
[As Jagger] "I'm not doing it."

00:05:44.601 --> 00:05:46.267
-So -- No.

00:05:46.267 --> 00:05:48.701
That's what he said,
-"No way! Go away!"

00:05:48.701 --> 00:05:52.067
-And so I go,
and I knock on the door,

00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:53.434
and he answers the door,

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:56.200
and he's wearing
a silk kimono robe...

00:05:56.200 --> 00:05:57.334
-[Normal voice]
Stop it. Come on.

00:05:57.334 --> 00:05:59.200
-...that is open to his navel.

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:01.501
-Yeah! Yes!

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:03.734
'Cause he can do that!
Because he's Mick Jagger!

00:06:03.734 --> 00:06:05.400
-Yes.
-I love that guy.

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:06.534
-Yes, he can.

00:06:06.534 --> 00:06:07.901
-Yeah, he's a fashion icon.
I love it.

00:06:07.901 --> 00:06:09.501
-Anyway, I had to go
into the suite,

00:06:09.501 --> 00:06:10.934
which it took us five minutes

00:06:10.934 --> 00:06:12.634
to walk through
all the many rooms

00:06:12.634 --> 00:06:14.300
to finally get
to the living room

00:06:14.300 --> 00:06:16.601
and sit down and convince him
to do the interview.

00:06:16.601 --> 00:06:18.033
-And did he do it?
-He did it.

00:06:18.033 --> 00:06:19.567
-Yeah. That's what I'm talking
about. 'Cause he's the best.

00:06:19.567 --> 00:06:20.934
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:20.934 --> 00:06:23.167
And you got to see him
in a kimono. You got a story.

00:06:23.167 --> 00:06:24.968
-He's really smart.
-Oh, he's brilliant.

00:06:24.968 --> 00:06:26.667
-He's a really smart guy.
-Brilliant.

00:06:26.667 --> 00:06:28.534
He knows exactly
what he's doing.

00:06:28.534 --> 00:06:31.000
And then the other story
that -- It touched me, actually.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:32.767
I thought it was beautiful,
this story.

00:06:32.767 --> 00:06:36.300
You interviewed Joe Biden
before he was president.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:37.767
-Right before he was president.

00:06:37.767 --> 00:06:39.100
I have interviewed him
many times.

00:06:39.100 --> 00:06:40.501
I interviewed him
and Lindsey Graham

00:06:40.501 --> 00:06:44.334
in the green zone in Iraq,
in Baghdad during the war.

00:06:44.334 --> 00:06:46.701
And that's when the two of them
were playing well together.

00:06:46.701 --> 00:06:48.033
-Wow.

00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:50.968
-But he wrote a beautiful book
called "Promise Me, Dad"

00:06:50.968 --> 00:06:53.868
about his son Beau Biden
who died of brain cancer

00:06:53.868 --> 00:06:55.767
and asked me to please
come down to Florida

00:06:55.767 --> 00:06:58.133
and interview him in front of
a live audience, which I did.

00:06:58.133 --> 00:07:00.367
And that's a picture
of us backstage.

00:07:00.367 --> 00:07:02.934
And, of course, I was --
At the end of the interview,

00:07:02.934 --> 00:07:05.334
I had to ask, are you
going to run for president?

00:07:05.334 --> 00:07:08.934
And it was right before
he went back up to Delaware

00:07:08.934 --> 00:07:10.467
to meet with the family
and decide.

00:07:10.467 --> 00:07:12.801
But he was great
because he went --

00:07:12.801 --> 00:07:15.567
he came backstage afterward
and he said --

00:07:15.567 --> 00:07:18.133
And he spent a lot of time
talking, and he said,

00:07:18.133 --> 00:07:19.934
"Can I call your sons?"

00:07:19.934 --> 00:07:21.434
And I was like, "What?"

00:07:21.434 --> 00:07:23.934
And he said, "Hand me your
phone. Call your son."

00:07:23.934 --> 00:07:26.167
-So I called my son Zachary.

00:07:26.167 --> 00:07:28.801
And he spoke for 15 minutes
on the phone,

00:07:28.801 --> 00:07:31.567
first with my son Zachary,
and then with my son Sam,

00:07:31.567 --> 00:07:33.667
basically saying, you know,
"Thank you for letting your mom

00:07:33.667 --> 00:07:36.067
come down here tonight
and interview me.

00:07:36.067 --> 00:07:37.801
and giving her up
for a school night,"

00:07:37.801 --> 00:07:39.534
because it was a school night.

00:07:39.534 --> 00:07:41.267
And, you know,
"I'd like to take you out

00:07:41.267 --> 00:07:43.000
for a Coke next time
I'm in New York."

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.968
Like, yeah, right.

00:07:44.968 --> 00:07:47.334
-Are you going to be
interviewing people

00:07:47.334 --> 00:07:49.167
on the news show?

00:07:49.167 --> 00:07:51.467
-Oh, yes.
It's mostly interview-driven.

00:07:51.467 --> 00:07:53.434
We're going to take --
It's an hour-long show

00:07:53.434 --> 00:07:54.934
from 6:00 to 7:00
East Coast time.

00:07:54.934 --> 00:07:56.801
And we're gonna take the
handful of the biggest stories

00:07:56.801 --> 00:07:58.400
and go really deep on them.

00:07:58.400 --> 00:08:00.467
And I love doing
live interviews, Jimmy.

00:08:00.467 --> 00:08:02.000
It's my favorite part
of my job.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:05.567
So I don't want to do just --
you know, be a prompter jockey,

00:08:05.567 --> 00:08:07.234
as they call it,
when you just do nothing

00:08:07.234 --> 00:08:09.300
but read story, story, story.

00:08:09.300 --> 00:08:10.934
-So on Monday
we're gonna see you live.

00:08:10.934 --> 00:08:13.400
-Yeah, we've got a lot of great
guests on Monday night already.

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.334
I'm afraid to tell
because I don't want to jinx it.

00:08:16.334 --> 00:08:18.200
-[As Jagger] No. You don't have
to tell. We all know who it is.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:22.501
Hey! Remember me?!
I'm wearing a kimono!

00:08:22.501 --> 00:08:24.133
Hello! Remember me? Hey!

00:08:24.133 --> 00:08:25.734
-He's never gonna
talk to me again

00:08:25.734 --> 00:08:27.534
now that I told that story.
-He will. He will. I love you.

00:08:27.534 --> 00:08:29.601
Congratulations.
Good luck on everything.

00:08:29.601 --> 00:08:31.334
Elizabeth Vargas, everybody.
-Thank you.

00:08:31.334 --> 00:08:32.834
-"Elizabeth Vargas Reports"

00:08:32.834 --> 00:08:36.000
premieres Monday at 6:00 p.m.
on NewsNation.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.467
More "Tonight Show"
after the break. Stick around.

